# Every literal is [1, 1], but literalWithHole = [1, , 1].    pushedTo.push(1)    popped.pop()    storedAnywhere[5] = 1    emptied.length = 0    lengthened.length = 5    delete elementDeleted[0]    ofLength = new Array(5)
# i = position() >>> 0; if (i < stored.length) { stored[i] = 1; other[i] = 1; if (i < popped.length) popped[i] = 1 }
# An array has no holes where its creator fills every position below the length and nothing that gets hold of it can make a hole. Then a position inside the length holds an element, and a read there needs a bound check and nothing else.
# An append leaves no hole, and neither does a store at a position that a test has found below the length of the same array, as long as the array never gets shorter.
# Each such array stands beside one that differs in the one thing that costs the proof: a hole in the literal, a store at a position that nothing compares with a length or that is compared with the length of another array,
# an array that is also popped, a store to `length`, a deletion, and a creator that makes a length and no elements. What a literal holds, and that its storage is untouched, stays proven
# RUN: valueflow-apply
# CHECK-LABEL: variable g0 name="literal"
